{
  "statement": "The question on the table is not whether AI can generate prototype code fast. It can. The question is whether your organization will do the hard thing afterward — throw that code away and rewrite it — or do the easy thing, which is ship it and move on. Bet on the easy thing. That is what the incentive structure of Product, Engineering, and Finance guarantees. Product wants features in 90 days. Finance wants the cost savings on the Q1 ledger. Engineering gets handed a codebase nobody authored and told to keep it running. The AI tool works fine. The organization around it does not. And for an established consumer software company — not a startup burning cash, not a team in crisis — the 90-day urgency is a political choice, not a market necessity. A low-risk consumer product does not have a competitive emergency that justifies accepting the downstream costs this debate is really about.\n\nHere is the first cost nobody is modeling. NewVantage Partners reported in 2022 that 87% of enterprise AI projects never make it past the pilot stage. Not because the technology failed. Because the organization had no integration roadmap, no ownership transfer plan, no budget for the next phase. An AI-prototyped MVP is structurally a pilot project. It produces code that works in a demo, passes a product review, and then fights your existing CI/CD pipeline, your test suites, your monitoring stack, and your deployment process for the next six months. An established company has all that infrastructure already. The AI-generated code was not written to respect any of it. You are not saving time. You are borrowing it from the engineers who will spend Q2 through Q4 retrofitting prototype code into a system it was never designed to fit.\n\nThe second cost is the knowledge void. Software maintenance research going back to Lehman's Laws has shown that 60-80% of maintenance effort is comprehension — reading and understanding code, not modifying it. When a human writes prototype code, the shortcuts are known. The author can explain why they hardcoded a value, skipped an edge case, or chose a particular data structure. When AI generates the code, that knowledge never existed inside your organization. Nobody prompted for the reasoning. Nobody documented the tradeoffs. The person debugging this system in month eight did not write it, did not prompt for it, and cannot reconstruct the logic behind it. In an established company with team rotations, reorgs, and normal attrition, that person is almost certainly not even on the original team. You have shipped a system that no human being can fully reason about. That is not validated learning. That is an opaque liability with a login page.\n\nFinance will love the Q1 numbers. Studies on AI coding tools show 30-55% faster task completion on isolated coding tasks. That looks like a 40-60% cost reduction on the MVP sprint. But Kruchten, Nord, and Ozkaya's research on technical debt economics shows that the interest rate on unaddressed debt compounds nonlinearly. AI-generated code is technical debt taken on at scale, all at once, with zero institutional understanding of the principal. The savings are real in Q1. The refactoring costs, the incident response, the hiring of engineers to understand what the AI wrote — those land in Q2 through Q4. And here is the kicker: Finance will never fund the rewrite, because the prototype is already shipping and generating user data. Product will never pause features for a rewrite, because the backlog is full. The deployment-capability gap — the distance between working prototype and production-grade maintainable software — is where the real bill accumulates. Nobody in the 90-day sprint is incentivized to present that bill.\n\nAI-generated code can accelerate validated learning under exactly four conditions: the organization treats it as disposable scaffolding explicitly marked for rewrite; human engineers review and take ownership of every module before it ships; Finance budgets upfront for a post-MVP rewrite cycle; and the validated learning is captured in user research and documentation, not in the code itself. Lean methodology — Ries, Gothelf — has always said the MVP's value is the hypothesis test, not the implementation. The code is the experiment apparatus, not the result. But Christensen's work on how organizations handle successful prototypes tells us what actually happens: the prototype ships, gets patched, gets inherited, and becomes the product. So the question every executive in this room should be asking is not \"can AI generate our MVP code faster?\" — the answer is obviously yes. The question is: who at your table represents the maintenance engineers who will inherit this code in month four, and do they have the political power to demand the rewrite that Product and Finance will never voluntarily fund?",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "🧊", "tooltip": "AI-generated code is an iceberg — the visible speed gain hides the mass of maintenance cost below the waterline."},
    {"symbol": "🔑", "tooltip": "The code ships without anyone holding the key to understanding it — the knowledge void locks the team out of their own system."},
    {"symbol": "⏳", "tooltip": "The 90-day sprint is an hourglass that front-loads savings and back-loads costs — but nobody flips it over to count the second half."}
