ated operational trigger, not a gatekeeping ritual.\n\nThe Skeptic correctly diagnoses that Product, Engineering, and Finance incentives misalign around the prototype-to-production transition. \"The prototype becomes the product\" is a failure mode as old as rapid prototyping itself. The 87% failure rate for enterprise AI projects past pilot confirms this. But here is what that statistic actually proves: the bottleneck is human organizational capacity, not the technology. Blaming AI-generated code for incentive misalignment is like blaming the printing press for bad editing. Established companies — the explicit scope of this debate — already have architecture review boards, planned refactoring cycles, and engineering investment budgets. The prescription is adaptive governance: bake a 15-20% rewrite budget into the original 90-day business case at sprint zero. Assign ownership transfer protocols before a single line of prototype code ships. Use automated verification gates as the governance layer. These are standard engineering management practices, not organizational transformations. The Skeptic is arguing that established companies are too incompetent to follow their own playbooks. That is fatalism dressed as analysis.\n\nOne more dimension the Skeptic's framework misses entirely: AI tools don't just create technical debt — they reduce the cost of servicing it. The same tools that generate prototype code also generate tests, documentation, and refactoring suggestions. If comprehension is 60-80% of maintenance cost, then AI-assisted comprehension tools attack the single largest line item in the maintenance budget. Google's internal data from 2023 showed that AI-assisted code review reduced review cycle time by roughly 30%. The interest rate on AI-generated technical debt is falling quarter over quarter as tooling improves. The interest rate on delayed deployment — lost user data, missed market windows, stale hypotheses — stays constant or rises. Treating AI coding tools as a one-shot generation input and ignoring their continuous lifecycle value is like evaluating electricity based only on the cost of wiring the first building. The Skeptic's technical debt calculation is static. The world is not.\n\nSo here is the decision for the executive in the room. You can ship in 90 days with AI-assisted prototyping, pair it with a pre-committed rewrite budget and automated verification gates, and start collecting the production user data that tells you whether this product is worth building at all. Or you can spend those 90 days in architecture review committees, ship nothing, learn nothing, and face the same organizational dysfunction the Skeptic warns about — except now you also have zero market signal. The Skeptic asks you to bet that your organization cannot govern a fast process. The accelerationist position asks you to bet that it can. For an established company with mature infrastructure, that is not a leap of faith. It is a leadership decision. And refusing to make it has a cost your Finance team will never put on a spreadsheet.",
