  "statement": "First, the source correction: the 87% pilot failure figure was VentureBeat 2019, not NewVantage Partners 2022. That's my error on attribution and I own it. The number still holds directionally — \"State of AI in the Enterprise: The untapped edge\" documents the same pattern, finding that only 26% of organizations can move beyond proof-of-concept to generate tangible AI value at scale. Three out of four established companies get stuck in what that report calls the \"proof-of-concept trap,\" funding cheap pilots while avoiding the harder work of scaling. So the failure pattern is robust across sources and years. And here's what makes it useful: the Accelerationist accepts this failure rate while simultaneously claiming established companies already possess the governance infrastructure — architecture review boards, planned refactoring cycles, engineering investment budgets — to execute their adaptive governance prescription. Pick one. If the infrastructure exists, why do 74% of companies choke at the scaling stage? The answer is that governance mechanisms exist on org charts but not in budget fights.\n\nThe strongest version of the Accelerationist's case goes like this: budget a 15-20% rewrite allocation at sprint zero, assign ownership transfer protocols so every AI-generated module has a named human owner by week six, and gate deployments through automated code quality checks. On a whiteboard, this is elegant. In a quarterly planning meeting, it's dead on arrival. Here's why: that 15-20% rewrite budget is a pure cost center. It produces no new features. It generates no user-facing metrics. Product leadership is measured on velocity and feature delivery. Finance is measured on growth per engineering dollar. Engineering leadership — the supposed natural champion — is evaluated on delivery speed and headcount efficiency, not code comprehension scores. So when the VP of Product walks into the Q2 planning session and says \"we need to redirect that rewrite budget to the engagement feature our competitor just shipped,\" name the person in the room who says no. Not the role. The person. Because \"AI Doesn't Reduce Work — It Intensifies It\" documents exactly this dynamic: companies focus on getting more employees to use AI tools, not on funding the downstream cleanup those tools create. The incentive structure actively suppresses the rewrite.\n\nThe claim that AI tools themselves reduce the cost of maintaining AI-generated code deserves scrutiny on its own terms. The argument is that AI-assisted code review, documentation generation, and comprehension tools lower the interest rate on technical debt. Translate that into what actually happens on the ground: an engineer who didn't write the code asks an AI tool to explain it. The AI tool produces a plausible-sounding explanation. The engineer has no independent basis to verify whether that explanation is correct, because the reasoning behind the code never existed in any human mind. This is not like using a linter or a compiler — those are deterministic tools that produce the same output every time. A stochastic comprehension tool can hallucinate an explanation that looks right and isn't. The debt doesn't shrink. It becomes invisible until something breaks in production at 2 AM and the on-call engineer is debugging code that nobody authored and nobody truly understands, with an explanation tool that might be wrong. That's not a falling interest rate — that's converting visible debt into hidden systemic fragility.\n\nThe Accelerationist argues that human-authored prototype code is equally opaque six months later, so the knowledge void isn't AI-specific. That's half right and entirely misleading. Yes, prototype code rots. Yes, documentation is always inadequate. But human-authored code has something AI-generated code structurally cannot: an author who once held the reasoning in their head. That person can be pulled into a code review. They can answer \"why did you use this