e budget to the engagement feature our competitor just shipped,\" name the person in the room who says no. Not the role. The person. Because \"AI Doesn't Reduce Work — It Intensifies It\" documents exactly this dynamic: companies focus on getting more employees to use AI tools, not on funding the downstream cleanup those tools create. The incentive structure actively suppresses the rewrite.\n\nThe claim that AI tools themselves reduce the cost of maintaining AI-generated code deserves scrutiny on its own terms. The argument is that AI-assisted code review, documentation generation, and comprehension tools lower the interest rate on technical debt. Translate that into what actually happens on the ground: an engineer who didn't write the code asks an AI tool to explain it. The AI tool produces a plausible-sounding explanation. The engineer has no independent basis to verify whether that explanation is correct, because the reasoning behind the code never existed in any human mind. This is not like using a linter or a compiler — those are deterministic tools that produce the same output every time. A stochastic comprehension tool can hallucinate an explanation that looks right and isn't. The debt doesn't shrink. It becomes invisible until something breaks in production at 2 AM and the on-call engineer is debugging code that nobody authored and nobody truly understands, with an explanation tool that might be wrong. That's not a falling interest rate — that's converting visible debt into hidden systemic fragility.\n\nThe Accelerationist argues that human-authored prototype code is equally opaque six months later, so the knowledge void isn't AI-specific. That's half right and entirely misleading. Yes, prototype code rots. Yes, documentation is always inadequate. But human-authored code has something AI-generated code structurally cannot: an author who once held the reasoning in their head. That person can be pulled into a code review. They can answer \"why did you use this