gment. But here is what happens to that budget in practice. Sprint three arrives. The gates are green. Coverage is at 82%. Complexity scores are below threshold. Product looks at the dashboard, sees no red, and asks why 15% of engineering capacity is allocated to fixing code that every metric says is fine. Finance backs them up because the quarterly plan assumed feature velocity, not rework. The budget gets reallocated. This is not speculation — it is Goodhart's Law applied to quality metrics, and it is the same pattern documented in \"State of AI in the Enterprise: The untapped edge,\" which found only 26% of organizations can move beyond proof-of-concept to generate tangible value at scale. The rest get stuck in a cycle of funding pilots rather than facing the harder work of integration and maintenance. A pre-committed budget is a political artifact. It survives exactly as long as the metrics support it. When the gates show green on structurally clean but semantically broken code, the political cover for maintaining that budget evaporates.\n\nThe Google SRE error budget and Netflix chaos engineering precedents are real, but they are misapplied here. Those systems were built by teams who wrote the code and designed the reliability targets. The engineers who set the error budget understood what failure modes they were protecting against because they had authored the system. With AI-generated prototype code, the team setting the error budget is governing a system nobody designed — they prompted it into existence. The epistemic relationship between the team and the codebase is different in kind, not degree. A team that never built the mental model of why the code is structured the way it is cannot set meaningful reliability targets for it. They are guessing at what might break. And as the \"trust attitudes and use of ai global report\" documents, even employees who encounter real-world inefficiencies frequently lack forums to communicate ground truth to decision-makers — the people closest to the code's actual behavior are often the last to be heard in planning conversations. So if the gates cannot see semantic debt, and the rewrite budget gets cannibalized when the gates show green, and the engineers closest to the code lack organizational channels to flag what they are seeing — who exactly notices the architecture is wrong before the 90-day MVP becomes the permanent system?",
