ing the \"organizational readiness probe\" — post-mortem analysis, failure-mode cataloging, error-budget calibration — which competes for the same engineering hours as the rewrite budget. Now compare that to the claimed 30-day acceleration. The governance framework that makes AI-generated prototyping safe is approaching the cost of just writing the code with a competent team. The net value isn't obviously positive. It might be negative. It's certainly within noise for any company that already has engineers on payroll.\n\nThe organizational readiness probe framing is the most honest thing in this debate — but only if it's actually a probe and not a euphemism for \"ship it and learn later.\" A real probe has pre-registered success criteria. What are the semantic health indicators that get measured before shipping? Who has the authority and the incentive to report that the containment boundary leaked? \"Sycophantic Chatbots Cause Delusional Spiraling, Even in Ideal Bayesians\" shows that even informed users remain vulnerable to over-trusting AI outputs — awareness of the problem doesn't fix the problem. A team running a knowledge deficit on code they didn't write, under automation bias they can't fully correct for, measuring health with gates that only catch structural properties — that's not a probe. That's a coin flip with a clipboard. So here's the question nobody wants to answer: if the probe reveals that the containment boundary leaked and semantic debt has already coupled to production users, who in the organization has the pre-committed authority to pull the plug — and when has that ever actually happened?",
